### Six core elements

| Element | Definition |  Your object |
|---|---|---|
| `title` | A name given to the resource. | mythological painting |
| `creator` | An entity responsible for making the resource. | Sandro Botticelli |
| `date` | A point or period of time associated with an event in the lifecycle of the resource. | 1445 – 1510 |
| `type` | The nature or genre of the resource. | painting |
| `subject` | A topic of the resource. | Renacimiento |
| `identifier` | An unambiguous reference to the resource within a given context. | 1890 n. 878 (Inventory Number); Q476036 (WikiData ID) |

### Optional core elements

| Element | Definition | Your object |
|---|---|---|
| `description` | An account of the resource. | The Birth of Venus is one of the most famous paintings by Sandro Botticelli. It depicts the goddess Venus arriving on the island of Cyprus, standing on a giant scallop shell after emerging from the sea. She is blown towards the shore by Zephyr and possibly Aura, while a young woman, identified as one of the Graces or the Hora of spring, waits to cover her with a flowered cloak. The painting celebrates Venus as a symbol of love and beauty and reflects the ideals of the Italian Renaissance. |
| `publisher` | An entity responsible for making the resource available. | Gallerie degli Uffizi, Florence, Italy |
| `contributor` | An entity responsible for making contributions to the resource. | Daniela Parenti (author of the artwork description) |
| `format` | The file format, physical medium, or dimensions of the resource. | Tempera on canvas; 172.5 × 278.5 cm |
| `source` | A related resource from which the described resource is derived. | [Uffizi Galleries – Birth of Venus](https://www.uffizi.it/en/artworks/birth-of-venus) |
| `language` | A language of the resource. | EN |
| `relation` | A related resource. | Primavera, Sandro Botticelli, Gallerie degli Uffizi |
| `coverage` | The spatial or temporal topic of the resource, spatial applicability of the resource, or jurisdiction under which the resource is relevant. | Florence, Italy; Italian Renaissance; 15th century |
| `rights` | Information about rights held in and over the resource. | Public Domain (original artwork) |


